"""PHP request boundaries and Perl text-processing conventions."""
from build import S, lesson as L

def php():
 return [
 L('runtime','PHP execution and request boundaries',
 'PHP runs in different server APIs, including CLI and web servers. A web request receives its own execution context; ordinary local variables do not persist across later requests. CLI arguments arrive through argv, while query and form values use superglobals. php -l checks syntax without executing the script. Keep declarations before use, and distinguish the language interpreter from the server that routes HTTP requests. These examples assume PHP 8.2 or later.',
 S('$ php ~-l~ app.php','Checks PHP syntax without executing the script','Starts a production web server|Runs every test','Lint mode checks syntax; it does not prove that names or runtime operations are valid.','-v|-i','What does this command check?'),
 S('// CLI invocation: php app.php ${word}\necho $argv[~1~];','${word}','app.php|0','Index one is the first CLI argument after the script name.','0|2','Given this invocation, what is printed?'),
 S('$name=$_GET["name"] ~??~ "guest";','Uses guest when the query key is absent or null','Persists the name across all requests|Validates the name as safe HTML','Null coalescing supplies a fallback, but validation and output encoding remain separate.','+|===','What does this expression guarantee?'),
 S('~session_start~(); $_SESSION["name"]="${word}";','Uses session storage to retain a value across requests','Makes a local variable permanent|Encrypts every response','Sessions provide a persistence mechanism; ordinary variables do not.','ob_start|header','Assume headers can still be sent: what mechanism is used?')),
 L('validation','Validate input and encode output',
 'HTTP input is external data, even when an HTML form declares a numeric field. A query key may be absent or array-valued. Validate shape before conversion, and compare filter failures strictly because valid zero is falsey. Escaping is output-context-specific: htmlspecialchars encodes HTML text, not SQL, JavaScript, or a complete URL. This lesson separates input validation from safe rendering and avoids teaching broad claims such as making a string universally safe.',
 S('$value=filter_var("0",FILTER_VALIDATE_INT); echo ($value ~===~ false) ? "invalid" : "valid";','valid','invalid|0','A valid integer zero must not be confused with the false failure sentinel.','==|!=='),
 S('$raw=["${word}"]; echo ~is_string~($raw) ? "text" : "reject";','reject','text|Array','Check shape before treating request data as a scalar string.','is_array|isset'),
 S('echo ~htmlspecialchars~("<${word}>",ENT_QUOTES,"UTF-8");','&lt;${word}&gt;','<${word}>|${word}','HTML encoding turns angle brackets into text entities for this output context.','strtolower|trim'),
 S('$name=null; echo $name ~??~ "${word}";','${word}','0|TypeError','The fallback handles null before the value reaches output.','+|===')),
 L('http','HTTP responses, forms, and redirects',
 'HTTP status and headers describe a response separately from its body. Set headers before output unless buffering deliberately delays it. header with a Location value requests a redirect but does not stop PHP execution; exit prevents later code from running. GET and POST values are separate inputs. A form changes the source of input, not its trustworthiness. These exercises read request/response boundaries without introducing a framework or a full authentication system.',
 S('~http_response_code~(404); echo "missing";','Sets the HTTP response status to 404','Redirects automatically|Throws an exception','The status setter communicates the failure separately from the body.','header|print','Assume a web request before headers are sent: what does this do?'),
 S('header("Location: /done"); ~exit~; echo "later";','The later body is not emitted','The later body is always emitted|The redirect is cancelled','exit stops execution after setting the redirect header.',';|print "continue"','What happens to the final echo?'),
 S('header(~"Content-Type: application/json"~); echo "{}";','Declares a JSON response body','Parses the incoming request JSON|Validates arbitrary PHP values','Content-Type describes the emitted representation; encoding still must be correct.','"Accept: application/json"|"Location: /json"','What does the header describe?'),
 S('$_GET=["name"=>"query"]; $_POST=["name"=>"${word}"]; echo ~$_POST~["name"];','${word}','query|Array','Reading POST explicitly chooses the form data in this fixture.','$_GET|[]')),
 L('json','JSON boundaries and explicit failure handling',
 'json_decode can produce objects or associative arrays depending on its second argument. JSON_THROW_ON_ERROR makes malformed input a JsonException rather than an ambiguous null result. Valid JSON null still decodes to PHP null. JSON encoding is serialization, not HTML escaping. At the boundary, check the expected shape and keys before relying on the data; parsing successfully does not establish the application-level schema.',
 S('$value=json_decode(\'{"size":${n}}\',~true~,512,JSON_THROW_ON_ERROR); echo $value["size"];','${n}','TypeError|0','The associative flag asks for arrays instead of stdClass objects.','false|null'),
 S('try { json_decode("bad",true,512,~JSON_THROW_ON_ERROR~); } catch (JsonException $e) { echo "invalid"; }','invalid','null|bad','The flag makes malformed JSON throw a catchable JsonException.','0|JSON_OBJECT_AS_ARRAY'),
 S('$value=json_decode("null",true,512,JSON_THROW_ON_ERROR); echo ~get_debug_type~($value);','null','false|array','JSON null is valid input and decodes to PHP null.','strval|count'),
 S('echo ~json_encode~(["size"=>${n}],JSON_THROW_ON_ERROR);','{"size":${n}}','[${n}]|${n}','The string-keyed array becomes a JSON object.','serialize|print_r')),
 L('database','PDO parameters and transactional boundaries',
 'PDO is PHP\'s standard database interface, provided by an extension with a driver for the selected database. These questions assume PDO_SQLite is available. Prepare statements with placeholders and bind data through execute rather than concatenating SQL. Parameters represent values, not table names or SQL keywords. Transactions group related writes; catch failures and roll back at the application boundary. SQLite is used for small fixtures, not as a claim that all database dialects behave identically.',
 S('$db=new PDO("sqlite::memory:"); $s=$db->prepare("SELECT :size + 1"); $s->~execute~(["size"=>${n}]); echo $s->fetchColumn();','${next}','${n}|0','execute binds the named value and runs the prepared statement.','fetch|closeCursor'),
 S('$db=new PDO("sqlite::memory:"); $s=$db->prepare("SELECT :name"); $s->execute(["name"=>"${word}"]); echo $s->~fetchColumn~();','${word}','0|Array','The placeholder is data, and fetchColumn reads the first result column.','rowCount|errorCode'),
 S('$db=new PDO("sqlite::memory:"); $db->exec("CREATE TABLE t(n INTEGER)"); $db->beginTransaction(); $db->exec("INSERT INTO t VALUES (${n})"); $db->~rollBack~(); echo $db->query("SELECT COUNT(*) FROM t")->fetchColumn();','0','1|${n}','Rolling back discards the inserted row in this transaction.','commit|lastInsertId'),
 S('$s=$db->prepare("SELECT * FROM items ORDER BY ~:column~");','Binds a value expression, not a column identifier','Safely substitutes any column name|Changes the table schema','Choose identifiers from a trusted allowlist; placeholders only represent data values.','name|size','Why does binding a column name not choose the sort column?')),
 L('modern-contracts','Modern PHP value and API contracts',
 'PHP 8 provides named arguments, match expressions, union types, and constructor property promotion. match compares strictly and must cover the input or provide a default; it does not fall through like switch. A readonly property permits initialization but prevents later reassignment. strict_types is declared per calling file and changes scalar argument coercion at that boundary; type declarations do not validate every property of external data.',
 S('$value="1"; echo match($value) {1=>"integer", ~default~=>"text"};','text','integer|UnhandledMatchError','match uses strict comparison, so string 1 does not match integer 1.','"other"|0'),
 S('class Limit {public function __construct(public ~readonly~ int $value) {}} $x=new Limit(${n}); $x->value=${m};','Error: cannot modify readonly property','${m}|Creates a new instance','After constructor initialization, the readonly property cannot be reassigned.','static|private','What happens at reassignment?'),
 S('function label(string $name,int $count):string {return "$name:$count";} echo label(~count: ${n}, name: "${word}"~);','${word}:${n}','${n}:${word}|ArgumentCountError','Named arguments bind by parameter name rather than source order.','"${word}", 0|"other", ${n}'),
 S('declare(strict_types=1); function size(int $n):int{return $n;} echo size(~"${n}"~);','TypeError: string passed to int parameter','${n}|0','This strict calling file does not coerce a numeric string into an integer parameter.','${n}|0','What happens at the call?'))]

def perl():
 return [
 L('cli','Perl one-liners and script input',
 'Perl is often used to inspect and transform text streams. -e supplies program text, -n wraps it in an input loop, and -p also prints the current record after each iteration. The diamond operator reads files named in @ARGV or standard input when no files are supplied. -c checks syntax, but compile-time BEGIN blocks still execute. Read shell quoting separately from Perl quoting, and avoid in-place editing until the transformation has been inspected.',
 S("$ perl ~-ne~ 'print if /${word}/' input.txt",'Prints only matching input records','Prints every input record automatically|Edits the file in place','-n supplies a loop while the explicit print selects matching records.','-pe|-e','What does this command do?'),
 S("$ perl ~-pe~ 's/${word}/X/g' input.txt",'Prints every input record after substitution','Changes input.txt in place|Prints nothing without an explicit print','-p adds a print for each input record; no -i flag is present.','-ne|-e','What output behavior is requested?'),
 S('# Invocation: perl app.pl ${word}\nprint $ARGV[~0~];','${word}','app.pl|0','@ARGV starts with user arguments, not the script name.','1|2','Given this invocation, what is printed?'),
 S('$ perl ~-c~ app.pl','Checks syntax but may execute BEGIN blocks','Never executes any Perl code|Runs the entire script normally','Perl has compile-time execution; -c is not a sandbox for untrusted scripts.','-p|-n','What does this check guarantee?')),
 L('text-pipeline','Record processing with split, map, and grep',
 'Perl gives scalar and list context different meanings. split produces fields, grep selects original elements, and map produces transformed elements. Their blocks use $_ as the current value and can accidentally mutate aliased inputs. chomp modifies its argument and returns the amount removed, not the cleaned string. Use explicit separators and preserve trailing empty fields when the format requires them. These are native text APIs, not implementations of algorithms.',
 S('my @fields=split /,/, "${word},${n}"; print $fields[~1~];','${n}','${word}|2','split produces two fields and index one selects the second.','0|2'),
 S('my @values=(${n},0,${m}); my @positive=~grep~ { $_ > 0 } @values; print scalar @positive;','2','3|1','grep retains the two positive original values.','map|sort'),
 S('my @values=~map~ { $_+1 } (${n},${m}); print $values[0];','${next}','${n}|2','map returns each block result in list context.','grep|sort'),
 S('my @fields=split /,/, "a,b,", ~-1~; print scalar @fields;','3','2|1','A negative limit preserves the trailing empty field.','0|2')),
 L('regex-repairs','Captures, literal patterns, and substitutions',
 'Perl regular expressions distinguish grouping, captures, and anchors. Named captures make fields easier to identify. \u005c\u005cQ and \u005c\u005cE quote metacharacters when matching external literal text. Check whether a match succeeded before using captures; otherwise an earlier successful match can leave misleading state. The /g modifier replaces all matches in substitution. Avoid assuming a pattern from another language has the same escaping or character-class behavior.',
 S('my $text="size=${n}"; if($text =__TILDE__ /size=(?<count>\\d+)/) { print $+{~count~}; }','${n}','size|0','The named capture contains the digit text.','missing|name'),
 S('my $text="${word} ${word}"; $text =__TILDE__ ~s/${word}/X/g~; print $text;','X X','X ${word}|${word} X','The g modifier applies the substitution to both matches.','s/${word}/X/|s/other/X/g'),
 S('my $literal="a.b"; my $text="axb"; print(($text =__TILDE__ /~\\Q$literal\\E~/) ? "yes" : "no");','no','yes|a.b','Quoting treats the dot as a literal character, so axb does not match.','$literal|a.b'),
 S('my $text="${n}x"; print(($text =__TILDE__ /~\\A\\d+\\z~/) ? "yes" : "no");','no','yes|${n}','The whole-string digit contract rejects the trailing x.','\\d+|\\A\\d+')),
 L('scope-state','Lexical scope, dynamic localization, and closures',
 'my creates lexical bindings, while local temporarily changes a package variable and restores it on scope exit. This distinction matters for special variables such as the input record separator $/. A closure captures a lexical variable and keeps its storage alive. our declares access to a package variable under strict; it does not make a lexical copy. Avoid accidental globals in scripts that may later be reused as modules.',
 S('my $n=${n}; {my $n=${m};} print ~$n~;','${n}','${m}|0','The inner my declares a distinct binding.','0|1'),
 S('our $mode="${word}"; {~local~ $mode="inner";} print $mode;','${word}','inner|0','local restores the prior package value when the block exits.','our|'),
 S('my $n=${n}; my $next=sub { ++$n }; $next->(); print ~$n~;','${next}','${n}|0','The closure updates its captured lexical variable.','0|1'),
 S('my $before=$/; {~local~ $/; } print(($/ eq $before) ? "restored" : "changed");','restored','changed|Undefined variable error','local temporarily changes the record separator and restores it afterward.','our|undef','What happens to the record separator after the block?')),
 L('file-boundaries','File handles, encodings, and process arguments',
 'Use three-argument open with a lexical filehandle so filename data is separate from the mode. An encoding layer decodes file bytes into characters; without it, length may count encoded bytes. Check open and close results for meaningful I/O failures. List-form system passes a program and arguments separately, avoiding shell parsing for the multi-element form. Its return value is an encoded process status, not stdout. The tutor never runs these displayed process commands.',
 S('open my $fh, ~"<:encoding(UTF-8)"~, "notes.txt" or die $!;','Reads the file and decodes UTF-8','Writes a UTF-8 file|Treats every byte as a separate character','The mode separates read intent and decoding from the filename.','">"|"<:raw"','Assume opening succeeds: how is text read?'),
 S('my $text="${word}\\n"; my $removed=~chomp~($text); print $removed;','1','${length}|${word}','chomp removes the trailing newline and returns the number of characters removed.','length|uc'),
 S('system ~"program", "${word};other"~;','Passes the semicolon inside one argument','Always runs two shell commands|Returns the program stdout','This multi-element list form keeps argument data separate from shell syntax.','"program ${word};other"|"program"','How is the semicolon handled in this invocation?'),
 S('open my $fh, "<", "missing.txt" ~or die $!~;','Reports an open failure using the OS error','Silently creates the file|Retries forever','The failure branch stops the script with the current OS diagnostic.','or print "ok"|or 1','Assume the file is absent: what does this boundary do?')),
 L('testing','Module contracts and Test::More',
 'A traditional Perl module declares a package and returns a true value when required. Exporter controls which names callers can import; keeping exports explicit makes dependencies readable. Core Test::More emits test results through TAP. is compares an expected scalar and is_deeply compares nested structures. Tests should exercise real contracts, including failures; compilation alone cannot check input parsing or context-sensitive behavior.',
 S('package Config;\nsub size { ${n} }\n~1~;','Supplies the true return value expected by require','Runs size automatically|Exports every function','Traditional require expects the loaded module to return true.','0|undef','What is the purpose of the final expression?'),
 S('use Test::More tests=>1;\n~is~(${n}+1,${next},"increments");','One passing test','One failing test|No tests run','is compares the actual and expected scalar results.','isnt|fail','What result does the test report?'),
 S('use Test::More tests=>1;\n~is_deeply~([${n},${m}],[${n},${m}],"values");','One passing test','Fails because references differ|No tests run','is_deeply compares the contents of the two array references.','is|isnt','What result does the test report?'),
 S('package Config;\nuse Exporter "import";\nour ~@EXPORT_OK~ = qw(size);\nsub size { ${n} }\n1;','Allows callers to request size explicitly','Exports size to every caller by default|Makes size private','EXPORT_OK advertises optional imports, while EXPORT lists default imports.','@EXPORT|@ISA','What import policy is declared?'))]
